## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design and reason about multi-cycle 3-valued constraint propagation, a common motif in policy engines and configuration management where Unknowns must be carefully propagated to avoid false constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene 3-valued logic: T>U>F ordering, ¬T=F, ¬F=T, ¬U=U; ∨/∧ as max/min.
- Implication as a→b = ¬a ∨ b.
- Temporal constraints: handling two cycles and cross-time implications.
- Backtracking propagation: prune values that would force a constraint to F.

## Code Example

```javascript
// Kleene three-valued helpers
const order = {F:0, U:1, T:2};
function not(x){ return x==='T'?'F': x==='F'?'T':'U'; }
function litOr(a,b){ return order[a] >= order[b] ? a : b; }
function litAnd(a,b){ return order[a] <= order[b] ? a : b; }
function implies(a,b){ // ¬a ∨ b
  const na = not(a);
  return litOr(na, b);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this to N predicates and K cycles while preserving polynomial-time propagation?
- Compare Kleene vs Priest-style three-valued logics for the same constraint set in terms of guaranteed satisfiability detection.

## Why This Is Asked
This tests reasoning with Kleene 3-valued logic in interdependent constraints, and the ability to design a propagation/backtracking solver. It also requires concrete verification on a small example.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued semantics for ∧, ∨, ¬, →, ↔
- Constraint propagation and backtracking with U values
- Handling cycles and termination guarantees

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal Kleene 3-valued helpers
const val = {T:2, U:1, F:0};
function not(x){ return x==='T'?'F': x==='F'?'T':'U'; }
function land(a,b){
  const table = {T:{T:'T',U:'U',F:'F'}, U:{T:'U',U:'U',F:'F'}, F:{T:'F',U:'F',F:'F'}};
  return table[a][b];
}
function lor(a,b){
  const table = {T:{T:'T',U:'T',F:'T'}, U:{T:'T',U:'U',F:'U'}, F:{T:'T',U:'U',F:'F'}};
  return table[a][b];
}
function imp(a,b){ return lor(not(a), b); }
function equiv(a,b){ return land(imp(a,b), imp(b,a)); }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to detect unsat when no U is possible?
- How would you scale to 10+ variables with more operators and cycles?
